WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.780
Biscuits, pancakes, cookies, cake, and&nbsp;
… a worldwide fungal infection. HBO's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.220
"The Last of Us" is a thrill ride for gamers&nbsp;
and non-gamers alike, but those in-the-know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:16.320
found some delicious surprises hidden in the&nbsp;
show's first two episodes. Spoilers ahead!

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.400
In "The Last of Us Part 2," Ellie&nbsp;
tells her girlfriend Dina that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.180
Joel's favorite movie franchise is a&nbsp;
cheesy fictional '80s action series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.509
and she wants to use it as an olive branch&nbsp;
to help ease their strained relationship.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:31.767
"What movie you guys gonna&nbsp;
watch? What's Joel into?"

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.400
"Uh, Curtis and Viper 2."

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.180
Later in the game, while taking shelter&nbsp;
with Dina and Jesse in a Seattle theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.680
Ellie notices a "Curtis and Viper 4"&nbsp;
poster on the wall in one of the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.680
rooms and makes a comment to herself&nbsp;
that it was one of Joel's favorites.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.960
In the show, fans will notice that the DVD&nbsp;
Sarah borrows for Joel on his birthday is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.060
"Curtis and Viper 2," a film that obviously&nbsp;
holds importance in their relationship. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.080
what's the plot? In "The Last of Us&nbsp;
Part 2," Ellie explains that it's about,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.420
"Two former commandos that go rogue to fight&nbsp;
bad guys. And I think in [Curtis and Viper 2],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.300
they go up against Russian spies&nbsp;
or something. [...] The young one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.540
Viper, he’s a trained ninja.&nbsp;
And he’s a complete badass."

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.000
"Oh, this is the one with the deleted scenes!"

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.800
"Yeah, imagine how bad those have to be."

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.820
This Easter egg makes the impact the&nbsp;
franchise has on Ellie and Joel in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.780
video game much more dynamic, as it&nbsp;
emphasizes what Ellie means to Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.140
and how she has become a surrogate&nbsp;
daughter to him after Sarah's death.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.220
Joel is eventually willing to share&nbsp;
his interests with Ellie — interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.520
that he once shared with Sarah,&nbsp;
whose absence he felt so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.580
that he could barely talk about her&nbsp;
in the original "The Last of Us."

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.740
When players wake up as Sarah in the video game,&nbsp;
they have the option to pick up a birthday card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.540
in her bedroom. The card reads "Congratulations!"&nbsp;
and features an illustration of a green cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.800
dinosaur wearing a party hat. Sarah says&nbsp;
that she forgot to give the card to her dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.160
but that worry is soon overshadowed&nbsp;
by the noises and chaos outside.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.740
In the show, there is a shot of Sarah's bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.460
and that same exact birthday card&nbsp;
can be briefly seen on her desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.660
underneath some art supplies. The inclusion&nbsp;
of the art supplies in the scene could mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.580
that Sarah went to the trouble of hand-making her&nbsp;
father's card, a neat detail left out of the game.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.560
Much like in the game, the shots of the birthday&nbsp;
card are not lingered on for very long. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.960
Sarah forgets to give it to&nbsp;
Joel after gifting him his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.840
repaired watch and falling asleep&nbsp;
while watching a movie together.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.760
Another neat detail from the opening: Sarah&nbsp;
is wearing a t-shirt for the fictional band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:32.220
"Halican Drops" in both the show and the game.&nbsp;
In both versions, the list of tour stops on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.000
back include several locations that will&nbsp;
be important to the story down the road.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.860
One of the most infamous scenes from the&nbsp;
original game comes when Tommy, Joel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.060
and Sarah are attempting to drive out of Austin&nbsp;
amidst the outbreak. When they end up downtown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.160
Tommy's truck is totaled by another vehicle&nbsp;
as the infected attack survivors and fires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.940
break out across town, which leads to&nbsp;
Sarah injuring her leg in the escape.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.620
Fans were likely expecting that very same&nbsp;
outcome to unfold in the show, given how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.820
pivotal this event is in "The Last of Us" and&nbsp;
the fact that the scene mirrors the perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.980
players experience in the game. Instead, the&nbsp;
writers changed the layout of the scene, opting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.540
to first fake audiences out with an oncoming&nbsp;
truck that just narrowly misses Tommy's truck.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.020
The chaos that flips Tommy's truck in&nbsp;
the show does not stem from another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.720
road-bound vehicle. Instead, a plane crashes&nbsp;
in close proximity to Tommy, Joel, and Sarah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.700
which leads to debris flying at the truck&nbsp;
and causing it to crash. The switch-up gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.420
the scene a fresh feel in an episode that&nbsp;
was largely faithful to the original game.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.700
Speaking of "faithful to the game" – here's&nbsp;
an easy-to-miss small detail that fans of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.080
game likely picked up on. When Joel is forced&nbsp;
to kill his infected neighbor with a wrench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.860
he discards it, likely out of disgust&nbsp;
for what he just had to do with it.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.880
But in the "The Last of Us" game&nbsp;
series, wrenches can be used as weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.680
and the player is forced to discard them all the&nbsp;
time, since they only have a limited number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.980
uses. So switching out Joel killing his neighbor&nbsp;
Jimmy for Joel killing his neighbor Connie was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.620
a fresh take that also nodded&nbsp;
at the mechanics of the game.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.080
The world of "The Last of Us" is plunged into&nbsp;
chaos after the cordyceps fungal outbreak. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.260
the game, it begins with infected&nbsp;
crops in South America. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.460
the show takes one of the main sources&nbsp;
of the spread — airborne spores — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.900
substitutes them with tendrils that grow out&nbsp;
of the infected. While it's unclear so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.260
there must be some explanation for how&nbsp;
cordyceps took over the world in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.522
show. Fan theories circulating online&nbsp;
suggest one possible answer — flour.

00:04:23.712 --> 00:04:25.632
"I was thinking we'd make some cookies!"

00:04:25.718 --> 00:04:27.038
"Chocolate chip?"

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.253
"Raisin."

00:04:28.253 --> 00:04:32.040
Joel and Sarah manage to avoid eating&nbsp;
flour-based products at least four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.700
different times in Episode 1. First, they&nbsp;
find they have run out of it at breakfast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.020
and make eggs instead of pancakes. Later,&nbsp;
they politely decline when their neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.460
offer them biscuits – the same biscuits&nbsp;
the infected neighbor ate. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.360
Sarah and Connie bake cookies together,&nbsp;
but Sarah doesn't take any because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.500
have raisins in them. Finally, Joel forgets&nbsp;
to pick up his birthday cake after work.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.440
Beyond that, the creators of the&nbsp;
show may have hinted at all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.948
this in "The Last of Us Podcast."&nbsp;
There, Craig Mazin mentions that,

00:04:58.948 --> 00:05:03.660
"Careful viewers of this episode will&nbsp;
be rewarded repeatedly because little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.680
bits of breadcrumbs have been planted that are&nbsp;
going to pay off later in interesting ways."

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.860
Plus, it just so happens that the&nbsp;
world's largest flour mill is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.634
Jakarta, which appears to be at the&nbsp;
center of the outbreak on the show.

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:17.887
"Jakarta. Where is that? Middle&nbsp;
East? Doesn't ring a bell."

00:05:17.887 --> 00:05:19.860
"Definitely a country. Maybe part of Asia?"

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.137
"Jakarta isn't a country."

00:05:21.137 --> 00:05:24.060
Fans will just have to wait and&nbsp;
see how these enigmatic hints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.280
play out in future episodes of "The Last of Us."

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.820
"Dad, you love biscuits."

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.620
"I do … But I'm on Atkins."

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.700
When Ellie awakens at the beginning of&nbsp;
Episode 2, a butterfly hovers above her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.500
This is a direct callback to various scenes in&nbsp;
the original game, where Ellie is constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.000
enamored by the beauty of the butterflies and&nbsp;
fireflies living outside of the quarantine zone.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.100
Butterflies become a symbol of hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.880
which mirrors the hope that the Fireflies&nbsp;
have in Ellie's resistance to the Cordyceps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.868
virus -- which they think may hold&nbsp;
the key to producing a viable vaccine.

00:05:57.868 --> 00:05:59.220
"A vaccine."

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.120
Any child born after Outbreak Day is&nbsp;
brought into a world of immense violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.980
and cruelty. When Ellie is falling in love with&nbsp;
nature, taking the time to read comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.880
or cracking jokes about the adults, it's&nbsp;
a reminder that she's still just a kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.260
trying to piece together some semblance&nbsp;
of fun amid a hollow and deadly reality.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.580
On top of all of that, before the events&nbsp;
of "The Last of Us Part II," Ellie gets a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.167
tattoo of a butterfly to cover up the chemical&nbsp;
burn she gave herself to cover her bite scar.

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:29.272
"Um, just so it's out there, I can't swim."

00:06:29.272 --> 00:06:33.660
In the original game, it's a running joke&nbsp;
-- and nuisance -- that Ellie can't swim. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.220
numerous missions, Joel is forced to push Ellie&nbsp;
across bodies of water using a wooden pallet.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.780
In "The Last of Us Part II," flashbacks&nbsp;
show that Joel teaches her to swim and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.340
she actually becomes quite skilled at it. She&nbsp;
even gets good enough to make a joke about her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.660
past shortcomings when the two encounter&nbsp;
a stray pallet near a museum in Wyoming.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.660
"Joel! Look! Wanna give me a ride?"

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.860
"Those days are long gone, kiddo."

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.380
When Joel, Tess, and Ellie&nbsp;
reach the hotel in Boston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.940
there is a lot of water separating them and&nbsp;
the stairwell they need to get to. Quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.600
Ellie notes that she can't swim because there&nbsp;
aren't many pools in the quarantine zone.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.400
Annoyed by her point, Joel shows Ellie that&nbsp;
the water only goes up to his knees. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.120
remains to be seen whether or not Ellie's&nbsp;
inability to swim will become as big of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.640
plot point in the show as it was in the game&nbsp;
-- or if pallets will ever come into play.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.940
In the game, as Joel and Ellie are about&nbsp;
to reach the hospital in Salt Lake City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:30.840
where the Fireflies are stationed, they&nbsp;
stumble upon a group of giraffes in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.040
open field. It's one of the most beautiful&nbsp;
and enchanting parts of the first game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.640
and it gives the player a break from&nbsp;
the never-ending brutality. Players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.820
can stand at the ledge and watch the&nbsp;
giraffes for as long as they'd like.

00:07:42.097 --> 00:07:47.137
"It's got its ups and downs. You&nbsp;
can't deny the view, though."

00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:51.900
In Episode 2 of the show, as Joel, Tess,&nbsp;
and Ellie are traveling across Boston and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.897
heading toward the Capitol building, they&nbsp;
stop and take in the sights from a rooftop.

00:07:55.897 --> 00:07:58.302
"You can't deny that view."

00:07:58.423 --> 00:08:02.220
Fragments of the same composition that&nbsp;
soundtracked the giraffe encounter in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.480
game are used to score this scene – a subtle&nbsp;
link between the game and the show. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.576
we'll hear it again (and see some&nbsp;
giraffes) in a later episode.

